{"purpose":"Record that re-publishing a line via `eka new \u003cid\u003e` + `publish` resets the object's state vector to the type default when the revision draft omits it, silently superseding an advanced state (e.g. a transitioned container returns to `planned`).","content":"`eka new` scaffolds a draft from the type template, carrying the type's DEFAULT owned state (for `ctr`: `containerState: planned`). Publishing that draft as a new instance makes it the highest instance (ADR-025), so the line's current state becomes the default again even when the previous instance had advanced (e.g. `active`). The revision draft does NOT carry forward the current state vector, and there is no warning. Observed during EKA v2 M6 orchestration: `ctr:solo-author` instance 1 = `active` (from the plan-lock container activation); an orchestration revision (to append the `sto:module-v2-path` work item) published instance 2 with no stateVector -\u003e current state reverted to `planned`, silently undoing the activation (the plan itself stayed `immutable`). Impact: a routine authoring edit can regress governed state and desynchronise the container from the locked plan. Options: (a) seed a revision draft with the line's CURRENT state vector; (b) refuse (or warn on) a publish that would regress an owned state; (c) document the hazard and mandate transition-after-revise ordering.","investigationSummary":"Found during EKA v2 M6 orchestration. Verified by reading `ctr:solo-author` instances 1 and 2 (`eka get eka/ctr:solo-author:1|:2`): v1 containerState=active, v2 containerState=planned; current (highest) = planned. Reproduced the empty execution projection this caused.","conclusion":"Authoring foot-gun: state-regressing publish is silent. At minimum needs documentation + a guard; ideally the revision draft carries the current state vector. Interacts with the `status` stale-instance finding (both stem from state/instance handling around revisions)."}